= i
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Constant functions give the lower bound $\aleph_2\leq\aleph_2^{\aleph_0}$. By the <generalized continuum hypothesis>, $\aleph_2=2^{\aleph_1}$. The <currying law for cardinal exponentiation> and infinite <cardinal arithmetic> therefore give
$$
\aleph_2^{\aleph_0}=(2^{\aleph_1})^{\aleph_0}
=2^{\aleph_1\cdot\aleph_0}=2^{\aleph_1}=\aleph_2.
$$
Hence $\boxed{\aleph_2^{\aleph_0}=\aleph_2}$.

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Put $\kappa=\aleph_\omega$. Apply <König theorem for cardinal numbers> to the sequences $\aleph_n<\aleph_{n+1}$:
$$
\kappa=\sum_{n<\omega}\aleph_n
<\prod_{n<\omega}\aleph_{n+1}\leq\kappa^{\aleph_0}.
$$
The sum is $\kappa$ because the countable <union> has <cardinality> at most $\kappa$ and its summands are cofinal in $\kappa$. Thus the power is strictly larger than $\kappa$, a fact that cannot be replaced by the regular-cardinal calculation of the preceding part.

For an upper bound, use $\kappa\leq2^\kappa$ and the <currying law for cardinal exponentiation>:
$$
\kappa^{\aleph_0}\leq(2^\kappa)^{\aleph_0}
=2^{\kappa\cdot\aleph_0}=2^\kappa=\kappa^+,
$$
where the last step is GCH at $\kappa$. There is no cardinal strictly between $\kappa$ and its successor, so
$$
\boxed{\aleph_\omega^{\aleph_0}=\aleph_{\omega+1}.}
$$

= iii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For $\kappa=\aleph_\omega$, monotonicity of <cardinal exponentiation> gives
$$
2^\kappa\leq\aleph_0^\kappa\leq(2^{\aleph_0})^\kappa
=2^{\aleph_0\cdot\kappa}=2^\kappa.
$$
The middle equality uses the <currying law for cardinal exponentiation>, and the final one uses $\aleph_0\cdot\kappa=\kappa$. Hence $\aleph_0^\kappa=2^\kappa$, even before assuming GCH. Applying the <generalized continuum hypothesis> yields
$$
\boxed{\aleph_0^{\aleph_\omega}=\aleph_{\omega+1}.}
$$

